__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"BOOKLET",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return t}}),e.getChapter=function(o){return t.find(t=>t.id===o)},e.chapterForTrigger=function(o){return t.find(t=>t.trigger===o)},Object.defineProperty(e,"BOOKLET_PARTS",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return o}});const t=[{id:'what_this_is',part:'basics',title:'What you are holding',hook:'Start here. Four paragraphs, then go outside.',pages:['You are a ninja of one of ten villages who woke in a ditch with no name. THE THREAD on the quest screen is your story: a week of learning your hands, then the contract with your description on it, to level 50 and the Ashen Court. Past it the world runs on clocks, and you get stronger by doing work in it. Everything in this booklet is a rule the game actually follows, including the parts that will kill you.','Four things carry you: the notice board gives you work, the wilds give you materials and corpses, the benches turn those into gear, and the guilds decide how good you are allowed to get at the benches. You can ignore any one of them for a long time. You cannot ignore three.','Nothing here is bought with money and nothing is bought with luck. Karma changes what the world offers you but never how hard you hit \u2014 that is a rule the game keeps, not a promise it makes.','The most useful habit: when a screen refuses you, read what it says. A control that will not work is required to tell you why, so the refusal is the instruction.'],see:['the_hunt','the_board']},{id:'levels_and_conditioning',part:'basics',title:'Levels, and the other kind of growth',hook:'Experience is the obvious one. It is not the important one.',pages:['Levels run to 150 and come from experience, and experience is worth more the further above you your opponent is. Fighting something four levels beneath you pays about a tenth of what fighting something above you pays. Grinding down is not slow, it is nearly nothing.','Conditioning is separate and does not come from experience at all. It comes from WHAT you fight: brutes and tanks build your vitality (HP), glass cannons and skirmishers build your agility (your pace: who moves first, how often you dodge, and escape), monsters and anything with an element sharpen your focus (chakra), humans teach you insight (your wits: reading their jutsu before they land, and finding the seam in their guard). HOW you kill adds its own: a swing or bare hands builds vitality, a kunai or body technique agility, ninjutsu focus, genjutsu insight, and a real beating toughens your vitality. The work levels you; your prey and your hands shape you.','This is why two ninja at the same level are not the same ninja. Somebody who only ever fought bandits reads people well and cannot take a hit. Crossing into a new band \u2014 30, 65, 125 \u2014 grants a permanent one-time jump in your pools on top of all of it.','Skills climb from use, and each is capped by your level, so you cannot train one to the sky at level 4. When a skill sits at its cap, the practice is going nowhere: level up, then come back.'],see:['the_hunt']},{id:'the_hunt',part:'basics',title:'How a fight is decided',hook:'Two numbers, and neither of them is your level.',trigger:'firstFight',pages:['Damage is your attack minus their armour, and it never goes below one. That floor is the whole game in one sentence. If your strike is under their armour, you are not doing reduced damage, you are doing exactly one \u2014 and a creature with 272 health will take 272 rounds to kill while it needs two to kill you.','So the question is never "what level is it". It is how many rounds each of you needs. The threat line under the enemy tells you the answer before you swing, and it is the one thing on that card no amount of ignorance hides. HOPELESS means your blade will not open it. Leave.','Your stance is chosen before the first blow and held to the end. Striking Fang hits a quarter harder and takes a quarter harder \u2014 a trade, not an upgrade, and against something that already two-shots you it is simply a faster death. Iron Shell hits softer, takes far softer, and breathes chakra back.','Analysing an enemy is worth real damage: knowing the element reveals the weakness, and a known weakness lands half again as hard. Scouting first gives them one free swing and tells you what you are looking at. Against something dangerous, one free swing is a cheap price. Against something hopeless it is a waste of a turn you should spend leaving.'],see:['the_body','the_wilds','gear_and_wear']},{id:'the_body',part:'basics',title:'Breath, food, and water',hook:'Three meters nobody explains, explained.',pages:['BREATH is your wind inside a fight. Every swing of the body spends it; guarding, bracing behind an item, or weaving a seal gives it back. Running empty never stops you \u2014 your arms just get heavy, and a heavy blow lands at about half. The rhythm is swing, swing, swing, breathe. A fed, watered ninja walks in with a deeper tank.','FED and WATERED are the two bars on your hub. They are buffs you keep up, never punishments you avoid \u2014 let them lapse and you are simply ordinary again. Fed: lessons stick harder, camp rest knits more flesh, and you walk into a fight with extra HP \u2014 +5% after one meal, +10% when the bar is half full or more. Watered: chakra returns easier at camp, the road tires you less, and you fight with extra chakra on the same terms. Any cooked dish feeds you; a water flask or herbal tea waters you. Meals STACK \u2014 eat three skewers before a long expedition and the bar climbs toward a full larder.','Food is preparation, not medicine. You eat from your pack, by a fire, before the work \u2014 in a fight your pack offers pills and elixirs, and the meal waits \u2014 salves and dressings wait too, for after, when a wound can be treated. Cold arithmetic: a pill mid-fight buys a round; a skewer bought the whole day.','BP on your hub is BANKED POINTS \u2014 free growth points from levels and rare elixirs, spent on the ninja sheet. What a point buys is printed next to each row and grows with the body spending it. They do nothing in the bank.'],see:['the_hunt','recipes']},{id:'outclassed',part:'basics',title:'When you are outmatched',hook:'The wilds do not scale to you. Read the ground.',trigger:'firstOutclassed',pages:['This is not a difficulty spike and nothing is wrong. The wilds get harder the further you walk from a village \u2014 roughly three levels per hex, all the way to 60 \u2014 and they do not care what level you are. You walked into ground that breeds things you cannot hurt.','The map shows it now. A hex outlined in gold breeds things well above you; a hex outlined in red and washed faintly with it breeds things you cannot damage at all. The tile card names the ground level against yours, and so does the banner inside the hex.','Walking away is a real move, not a failure. The more badly you are outmatched, the better your odds of breaking clean \u2014 almost certain when the read is hopeless. Fleeing pays nothing: no experience, no loot, no conditioning. The wasted trip is the whole punishment, and it is enough.','If you fail the break, it gets a free swing, so do not try it at a sliver of health when you could have left two rounds ago. The game will let you die out here. It just will not let it happen quietly.'],see:['the_wilds','dying']},{id:'dying',part:'basics',title:'Going down',hook:'It costs you. It does not erase you.',trigger:'firstDeath',pages:['Death is a setback with a recovery path, not a deleted character. You come back weakened and you have to mend, and the walk you died on was wasted \u2014 that is the cost.','What death does not do is take your knowledge. Everything you had analysed, every recipe you had been taught, every rank you had earned is still yours. The village does not un-teach you because a bird got the better of you.','The commonest cause of death in this game is not a hard fight. It is a fight that was never winnable, entered because the level number did not look alarming. Read the threat line.'],see:['outclassed']},{id:'the_board',part:'field',title:'The notice board',hook:'Four offers. Four hours. Every village its own wall.',trigger:'firstBoard',pages:['Each village wall holds four pieces of work and turns over every four hours on its own clock. It is not random per player and it cannot be re-rolled by closing the screen \u2014 the wall is a property of the village and the hour, so everyone standing in front of it reads the same thing.','The wall only posts work worth posting. Anything more than about four levels beneath you is dropped, so the board gets MORE useful as you grow rather than filling up with granary rats. Work above your level is not offered at all until you are ready for it.','Ranks D through S set the payout: an S contract pays three times what a D pays for the same amount of hunting. Renown comes with it, and renown is what the village remembers you by.','Most hunts point you outside the walls at a specific creature that dens in specific ground. The card names the terrain and the bearing. A bounty you can complete without leaving the village is the exception, not the rule.'],see:['the_wilds']},{id:'the_wilds',part:'field',title:'The ground outside',hook:'Three levels a hex, and it does not stop.',pages:['Distance from the nearest village is the only difficulty dial in the overworld, and the rate is about three levels per hex, capped at 60. Two hexes out you meet levels 2 to 7. Six hexes out, the teens and twenties. Fourteen out, the forties and fifties. There is no level-scaling and no rubber band: the ground does not care what level you are.','Terrain decides WHAT lives there \u2014 forest, desert, coast, mountain, snow, cavern and the roads all keep different company \u2014 and the ground level decides how big it is. A creature far above the ground it stands on will not appear there at all, which is why the deep wilds feel consistent instead of random.','Marching takes real time, about three minutes a hex, and the road can be ambushed. Inside a hex there are sixty-one smaller pieces of ground to walk and search, each on its own cooldown, and searching is where materials, points of interest and the Hollow come from.','Tracking will name the ground and the bearing for a creature you have taken work on. It is faster than wandering and it is the intended way to fill a bounty.'],see:['outclassed','skinning']},{id:'gear_and_wear',part:'field',title:'Gear, wear, and repair',hook:'Everything you own is running down.',trigger:'firstBrokenGear',pages:['Weapons wear every time you swing and armour wears every time you are hit. Nothing is permanent and nothing warns you twice. Gear that gives out mid-hunt is how a survivable fight becomes a death.','You can pay a smith, or you can mend it yourself \u2014 and how much you can restore is read off your own smithing. Novice hands get back less than half; a real smith at level 48 restores the whole thing. Repairs are paid in steel ingots either way.','Rarity decides how many sealing tags a piece will hold, and gear you FORGED YOURSELF holds one more than the same rarity found in the world. A blade made by your own hands has room the found one does not.','Sets matter more than individual pieces. Complete a set and it pays a bonus; god sets are the only source of percentage damage in the entire game, and they are earned, never bought.'],see:['sealing','the_benches']},{id:'sealing',part:'field',title:'Sealing tags',hook:'Small permanent bites, stacked carefully.',trigger:'firstSeal',pages:['A sealing tag is inscribed onto a piece of gear and stays there. How many a piece will hold depends on its rarity \u2014 and on whether you made it, which is worth one extra slot.','Tags combine. Three of a tier fold into one of the tier above for a small fee, so the low tags you have too many of are never waste. The tier a piece can carry is capped by the piece, so a rag will not hold a masterwork tag no matter how many you fold.','Divine seals are their own thing: three fragments and a serious fee at a sealmaster, and the fragments come from the worst things in the world.'],see:['gear_and_wear']},{id:'the_benches',part:'craft',title:'The eight trades',hook:'You already have all eight. They all start at one.',trigger:'firstBench',pages:['Smithing, alchemy, skinning, tanning, leatherworking, cooking, sealcraft and woodworking. Every character has all eight from the first day and every one of them starts at level 1. There is no trade to pick and none to miss out on.','The work itself is the training. You level a trade by making things with it, and the early rungs come fast \u2014 the second named rung is about forty-six crafts away, not four hundred. Trades cap at 60.','Knowing a recipe and being able to work it are two different things, and a bench will always tell you which one you are short of. Skill you earn by working. Drawings come five ways: the bench teaches some for tuition once your hands qualify, your guild teaches some by rank, some are sold as scrolls in limited numbers, some drop off things that did not want to give them up, and a few you simply work out yourself at the bench.','Guild membership boosts the trade that guild owns, and the boost grows with your tier inside it. That boost is the reason to join, and it is the only thing you lose by leaving.','You are never refused at somebody else\'s bench. Any guild will let an outsider work at their facilities using their recipes at your own skill level \u2014 for three and a half times the fee. Being a member is free access, not exclusive access.'],see:['recipes','guilds','skinning']},{id:'skinning',part:'craft',title:'From the corpse to the coat',hook:'Four steps, in the order leatherworkers actually use.',trigger:'firstCorpse',pages:['Only beasts can be skinned \u2014 there is nothing to take off a ghost. What you get depends on the creature, on your skinning level, and on what you are holding: a proper blade takes clean panels where bare hands tear everything. A herb sickle is worse than a tanto and far better than fingers.','What comes off a body is a raw PELT, not leather. Then: salt it, tan it, work it. Salting is the step people skip and it is the one that pays \u2014 a salted skin yields twice as much as an unsalted one, because nothing is lost on the walk home. That is the real reason every tannery in history kept a barrel of rock salt.','Tanning turns the salted skin into a HIDE. Leatherworking turns hides into gear. Each step is a different trade, and a chain is only as fast as the trade you have neglected.','The fur branch is separate: dressing a skin with the hair left ON, which wants tallow and a fussier hand. Leather takes the hair off. Fur keeps it. They are not the same craft and they do not make the same things.'],see:['the_benches','recipes']},{id:'recipes',part:'craft',title:'How you learn to make things',hook:'Rank teaches you. Bosses teach you the rest.',pages:['Basic recipes come with rank. Every promotion inside a guild teaches you what that rank knows, and once taught you keep it \u2014 leaving the guild costs you the bench discount, never the knowledge. Nobody can un-teach you.','The better recipes are blueprints, and blueprints drop. Dungeon bosses and the worst things above level 100 carry them. That is deliberate: the deep content is how the crafting ladder opens, so the two halves of the game pull on each other.','A recipe you cannot make yet says what is missing \u2014 the skill level, the rank, or the blueprint. Read the reason rather than guessing.','Master-crafted gear can require the hands to wear it, and a maker can always wear their own work. Nothing that DROPS is ever craft-gated: a corpse will never hand you something you cannot use.'],see:['the_benches','guilds']},{id:'guilds',part:'people',title:'Guilds',hook:'Join for the boost. Leave when it stops paying.',trigger:'firstGuild',pages:['A guild owns a trade and a building. Membership makes that trade better and that building free. Promotion is not time served \u2014 it reads your actual skill in the trade, so you cannot wait your way up.','Each promotion teaches you the recipes of that rank, and that knowledge is yours permanently.','You may leave. It costs a fee that grows with the tier you are abandoning, and there is a day\'s cooldown before you can join anywhere. The cooldown exists so that guild membership is a commitment rather than a switch you flip at each bench.','You keep using every other guild\'s facilities either way, at your own skill, for a higher fee. Nothing in the crafting chain is ever locked behind a door you cannot open.'],see:['the_benches','clans']},{id:'clans',part:'people',title:'Clans and war parties',hook:'Five ranks, real permissions, and somewhere to put them.',pages:['A clan has five ranks and each rank carries actual permissions rather than a title. Somebody has to be trusted with the treasury and somebody has to be trusted to march.','War parties march on the world: three minutes a hex, a short cooldown between outings, and bosses that stay dead for eighteen to thirty-six hours before the ground makes another. The world gods regenerate on a two-day clock and are the largest thing in the game.','Allies can be sworn to you and fight at your side, and some of them will only follow somebody who can actually make things. Their stamina grinds down across a fight and across the days.'],see:['guilds']},{id:'time',part:'people',title:'Clocks, and being away',hook:'The world keeps running when you close it.',pages:['Almost nothing here is a stored countdown; it is all timestamps. Boards turn over on the hour they turn over, cooldowns end when they end, and closing the app changes none of it. There is nothing to keep open and nothing to leave running.','Resting at a tavern heals you over time for a small fee, and the rest carries an experience bonus for a while afterwards. Hired hunters will farm for you once you are past level 30, after half an hour of quiet, up to eight hours\' worth \u2014 and they will skin at YOUR skinning level, badly, about a third of the time. If you cannot skin, neither can they. You cannot hire out what you never learned.','You will get a notification when something you were waiting on finishes, so there is no reason to sit and watch a timer. The intended shape of a session is to set something running, leave, and come back to a village that has moved on without you.']}];const o=[{key:'basics',label:'THE BASICS',blurb:'What the game is and how a fight resolves.'},{key:'field',label:'IN THE FIELD',blurb:'Work, ground, gear and the things that wear out.'},{key:'craft',label:'THE TRADES',blurb:'Seven skills, the hide chain, and the five roads to a recipe.'},{key:'people',label:'OTHER PEOPLE',blurb:'Guilds, clans, and the clocks the world runs on.'}]},926,[]);
